1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,030
A car loan with $25,000 left, at 5.2%.

2
00:00:06,075 --> 00:00:07,415
Pay it off early,

3
00:00:08,175 --> 00:00:10,055
or invest the extra cash?

4
00:00:10,450 --> 00:00:15,310
We ran both roads through all 48 months,
taxing every lot.

5
00:00:15,850 --> 00:00:22,300
The answer flips at an average annual
return of 6.11%, compounded, in the

6
00:00:22,350 --> 00:00:24,010
22% tax bracket.

7
00:00:24,850 --> 00:00:27,630
Below it, paying off ends ahead.

8
00:00:28,090 --> 00:00:29,870
Above it, investing does.

9
00:00:33,250 --> 00:00:34,590
Here is the setup.

10
00:00:35,050 --> 00:00:41,070
The rules here are US tax rules, and every
input is stated, not forecast.

11
00:00:41,650 --> 00:00:46,040
Loan interest is treated as not
deductible, so the model ignores the

12
00:00:46,090 --> 00:00:50,130
new-car loan interest deduction for 2025
to 2028.

13
00:00:50,650 --> 00:00:57,650
The loan has $25,000 left at 5.2%, with 48
monthly payments of $578.

14
00:00:59,050 --> 00:01:03,290
On top of that, $400 a month is available.

15
00:01:03,470 --> 00:01:05,450
That amount is illustrative.

16
00:01:06,675 --> 00:01:08,915
Road A sends it to the loan.

17
00:01:09,355 --> 00:01:12,795
Road B pays the minimum and invests it
every month.

18
00:01:13,450 --> 00:01:16,430
Both roads spend the same cash each month.

19
00:01:16,730 --> 00:01:18,230
Only the order changes.

20
00:01:18,850 --> 00:01:20,650
First, the certain part.

21
00:01:21,250 --> 00:01:24,070
Road A pays off the loan at month 28.

22
00:01:24,690 --> 00:01:27,110
Road B keeps paying through month 48.

23
00:01:27,850 --> 00:01:31,830
That leaves 20 months with no car payment
on road A.

24
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:38,890
Interest paid falls from $2,744 to $1,554.

25
00:01:39,850 --> 00:01:44,310
Road A avoids $1,190 of interest.

26
00:01:44,590 --> 00:01:47,790
That does not depend on markets or taxes.

27
00:01:48,250 --> 00:01:49,750
It is certain.

28
00:01:50,650 --> 00:01:57,180
Because both roads spend the same cash,
road A ends up investing exactly $1,190

29
00:01:57,230 --> 00:01:59,310
more than road B.

30
00:01:59,650 --> 00:02:04,460
So the question is narrow: can road B's
head start in the market beat a certain

31
00:02:04,510 --> 00:02:07,550
$1,190, after tax?
